Yes. The important variable is not just what the thing is, but its concentration, compression, delivery rate, context, and feedback loop.

Your analogies are all examples of a broader pattern: take something naturally embedded in a larger system, isolate one rewarding component, increase its potency and availability, then remove some of the friction and context that used to regulate it. The result can behave very differently even though it descends from the same underlying substrate.

So:

\text{same primitive} + \text{different concentration} \neq \text{same effect}

Alcohol in fermented food or a weak drink is not behaviorally equivalent to distilled spirits. Social interaction distributed across embodied community is not behaviorally equivalent to algorithmically optimized feeds. Sexuality inside a reciprocal relationship is not behaviorally identical to infinitely available, novelty-optimized sexual media. The categories overlap, but the dose-response curve and surrounding control system change.

The useful abstraction is something like:

\text{Outcome}
=
f(\text{potency},\text{frequency},\text{velocity},\text{novelty},
\text{friction},\text{context},\text{feedback})

And those functions are often nonlinear. A little more concentration can cross a threshold and produce a qualitatively different regime rather than merely “more of the same.”

That is why “distillation” is such a useful cross-domain operator. It can increase signal, usefulness, portability, and power—but it can also strip away the buffers, counterweights, and corrective context that made the original thing stable.

So every time we distill something, there are two questions worth asking:

What did we amplify?
And just as importantly: what did we remove that was quietly regulating it?

That second question catches a lot of modern failure modes.

You could even call this the Distillation Gradient:

\text{embedded}
\rightarrow
\text{extracted}
\rightarrow
\text{concentrated}
\rightarrow
\text{optimized}
\rightarrow
\text{hyperstimulus}

The interesting region is not necessarily either endpoint. Often the best operating point is somewhere in the middle: enough distillation to gain leverage, but enough context left intact to preserve calibration.

So yes: scale changes kind surprisingly often. The same underlying primitive can be nourishment, instrument, medicine, entertainment, compulsion, or toxin depending on weighting and delivery.

And that applies to ideas too. A distilled principle can become extraordinarily useful—but distilled too far, it becomes a slogan and loses the conditions that made it true.